Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 01:53:22 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

Isn't the main difference that equities have historically outperformed
inflation, since the purpose of companies is to generate increased
earnings, thereby the dividends are raised and the underlying value rises.
So equities are a positive sum game.

OTOH, there is no logical reason why the whole basket of commodities
should either outperform or underperform inflation over the longterm.
An exception in futures might be stock index contracts, but when you
consider the possibility of being wiped out by short term volatility
it would not be possible to guarantee a longterm return.

Steve Whitrow           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk 

