Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 21:07:29 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Nations as PPLs

The present international system is an intersting model for what a
world of PPLs might be like!

There are 150 or more countries.  Each of them could be considered a
PPL, each with a particularly complicated contract ("constitution").
One (effectively) consents to a contract by the fact of being on the
land owned by a PPL.

The various PPLs compete in an open market against each other for the
patronage of customers ("citizens").

Interestingly, many PPLs actively discourage new customers
(immigration controls).  This tends to leave many people at the mercy
of PPLs they'd rather not be citizens of, but everybody needs some
place to stand and some sort of economy in order to eat.

One problem with this model is that it is hard to found a *new* PPL,
because all of the dry land is occupied by the existing PPLs.  (This
is a market imperfection called "high barrier to entry" and tends to
prevent the usual benefits of a free market.)

(Actually, a new PPL, called "Minerva" was founded a few years ago
when some people dumped a load of fill on a shallow underwater
seamount.  Unfortunately, it was conquored by the PPL of Fiji, and
since it had not arranged a mutual-support pact with any other PPL, it
had no effective defense against attack.)

Dale

