Yes, impressive aren't they?

But in a sense, anything that would have a seal would automagically be
a deal between rather wealthy people.  The question is how did the
common people live?  Unfortunately, they didn't leave much
documentation, however they did it.

   Admittedly this may all be a misinterpretation -- but I don't think it is.

   I would suggest that if your sources actually say what you claim they do,
   they strongly conflict with everything I've read and seen.

Yes, but I have my suspcions that what one reads has been colored by
our culture's total acceptance of buying, selling, prices, etc. as
natural activities.  I suspect that the past was much like a feudal
manor -- a system of interlocking obligations; rents and taxes payed
in kind and labor, and fixed by custom; artisans supported by the
lord, whose production was distributed by the lord; a few major
transactions handled by contract; and a few unusual or luxury goods
traded by money or barter.  A system where rather little of everyday
life ever deals with a market transaction.

   I suppose I'm going to have to go out and start digging up references.

An interesting claim that should be independently checkable is
McEverdy's claim that pre-money cities had no marketplaces.

