Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 18:35:15 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities 

I do not know what Perry means by "zero-sum", and he has so far chosen
not to be explicit.  But I think the issue of whether or not "bets"
are worse than other investments like stocks because they are
"zero-sum" is very important, and so will explain for the benefit of
the rest of you.

Sometimes one assets can be "split" into a set of different assets
which can be later "joined" back up into the first.  One example is a
"basket" stock index, such as the S&P500, defined to be a certain set
of stocks.  You can take a bunch of stocks and make something work
exactly the same as such a basket.  

Another example is a set of state-contingent assets.  You can take $1
and split it into two superbowl "bets", "$1 if my team wins" and "$1
if your team wins".  These two assets can be joined back into $1.

Or even more familiar, a $10 bill can be split into two $5 bills.

The sum of the prices of the split up items must equal the price of the
joined up unit.  Arbitrage ensures this.  Thus holding any one of
these split items is a "zero-sum" investment relative to holding the
unit, since the price of one item can only go up as a percentage of
the unit at the expense of the percentage prices of the other items.

But any asset can be placed into some basket or another, so any asset
can be seen as "zero-sum" relative to some basket.  Just as the
football "bets" above are zero-sum relative to the $1, an individual
stock (in the S&P500) is zero-sum relative to the S&P500.

You might make a distinction based on whether the unit asset gives a
positive expected rate of return, and denigrate the football bets
relative to stocks on the basis that $1 bills don't give interest.
But we can split a S&P500 basket up into "S&P500 if my team wins" and
"S&P500 if your team wins", so that the units have the same return for
the two cases.

Another sense of "zero-sum" is whether some item is rationally more
valuable to you than the market price.  Assets with state-contingent
value (almost everything) can be more valuable if your beliefs about
the chances of future states differ from the market consensus.  Assets
held for this purpose alone, and not because the item is actually more
directly useful to you than someone else, are often called "bets".

But by this definition almost everyone buys individual stocks as bets,
since those stocks are mainly used for investment purposes.  In
contrast, commodities futures are often bought because individual
people and firms can gain special value from the actual commodities
represented.

Thus I again claim, stocks are no less "zero-sum" than futures or bets.

Robin Hanson

